<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jin–Song wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1125">1125</a>–<a href="/years/1234">1234</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jin–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> wars were a series of conflicts fought from 1125 to 1234 between the Jurchen-led Jin dynasty and the Song dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingkang-incident">Jingkang incident</a> of 1127 the Jin took the Song capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> and captured two emperors, forcing the Song court south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>. Generals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-fei">Yue Fei</a> resisted, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shaoxing">Treaty of Shaoxing</a> (1142) fixed the border on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a>; later wars changed little before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed the Jin in 1234.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jin-song-wars.jpg" alt="Jin–Song wars" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ian Kiu · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jin_Dynasty_1141_(no_borders).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
